# orphan.poke -- spawn a child that will never finish, then exit anyway.
#
# THE DEADLOCK THIS EXISTS TO FORBID, and it was real. A spawned child
# inherited the write end of the INTERPRETER's stdin, because child.c made
# those two pipes with a plain pipe() and nothing marked them close-on-exec.
# So when bs_child_finish closed the broker's copy to give the interpreter end
# of input, the interpreter did not get it: the grandchild was still holding a
# writer open.
#
# Three processes, all blocked, none of them wrong by its own lights. The
# interpreter waits on ',' for input that cannot arrive. The broker waits in
# waitpid for the interpreter. The grandchild waits to read a stdin nothing
# will ever write to. Nobody prints anything. It was found from bfsodium,
# where a program sent its exit frame, got its reply, and then sat for fifteen
# minutes.
#
# What makes this fixture catch it is that the child is DELIBERATELY LEFT
# RUNNING. drive.poke writes to its child, closes the pipe, waits for it and
# collects its status -- a tidy conversation, and tidy is exactly what hides
# this. Here the child is spawned with a pipe it will block on for ever, and
# the program exits without writing to it, without closing it and without
# waiting. The broker must still come back.
#
# The suite runs it under a bound. A pass is "this terminated"; there is no
# output to compare, because the point is the absence of a hang.
#
# Run with the broker's working directory set to a scratch directory holding
# the interpreter and echo.bf, the same way drive.poke is.
#
# Frames transcribed by hand from ABI.md sections 3, 7.14 to 7.16.
#
#   hello  op 01, len 10
EMIT 01 0a 00 42 53 54 4d 01 00 00 00 00 00
READ 107
#   pipe  op 0e, flags 0.  Handles 4 (read) and 5 (write).
EMIT 0e 02 00 00 00
READ 11
#   spawn  op 0f, len 0x30 = 48.  dir ffffffff, flags 0, nfdmap 1, nargv 2,
#   nenv 1, reserved 0.  The child's stdin is handle 4 and it gets nothing
#   else -- no stdout, so anything it printed would go nowhere.
EMIT 0f 30 00 ff ff ff ff 00 00 01 02 01 00
EMIT 00 04 00 00 00
#     "bfi", argv "bfi" "echo.bf", env "BFI_EOF=zero"
EMIT 03 00 62 66 69
EMIT 03 00 62 66 69
EMIT 07 00 65 63 68 6f 2e 62 66
EMIT 0c 00 42 46 49 5f 45 4f 46 3d 7a 65 72 6f
#     the reply is a process handle: 6
READ 7
#   AND NOW LEAVE. Handle 5 is not written to, not closed, and handle 6 is
#   never waited on. echo.bf is blocked reading a pipe whose only writer is
#   the broker, which is about to stop being interested.
#
#   exit  op 02, len 1, code 0
EMIT 02 01 00 00
READ 3
#   AND THEN READ ONE MORE BYTE, which is the whole test.
#
#   The broker answers the exit frame and then closes this program's stdin so
#   that a program blocked on ',' can finish. A fixture that STOPPED here
#   would never test that: it would run off the end of its instructions and
#   exit on its own, needing no end of input at all -- which is exactly what
#   the first version of this file did, and it passed whether the pipes were
#   close-on-exec or not.
#
#   So ask for a byte that is never coming. Now the program can only finish if
#   the close actually reaches it, which it does not when a grandchild is
#   holding the other end of that pipe open.
READ 1
